{"model": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q4_K_M.gguf", "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Extract a compact state card from the selected raw sessions.\nUse only the selected sessions and the query. Do not infer hidden metadata or\nadd facts outside the evidence.\n\nSelected evidence sessions:\n<evidence_session_01>\n[user] I’m thinking about getting into wearable tech—especially smartwatches or fitness trackers—but I’m overwhelmed by the options. If you had to compare the big ecosystems (Apple Watch, Garmin, Fitbit, Samsung, etc.), what are the practical differences in day-to-day use, and what should I prioritize if I care about health tracking and convenience?\n[assistant] If you look at the big ecosystems in everyday use, the differences mostly come down to how tightly each watch integrates with your phone, how reliable the health data is, and how long the battery lasts.\n\nApple Watch feels the most seamless if you’re already in the iPhone world. Notifications, calls, payments, and apps just work without much fiddling. Health tracking is solid and improving every year, especially for heart metrics and activity trends, but the battery usually lasts only a day or so. It’s the most convenient option but not the longest‑lasting.\n\nGarmin leans heavily toward fitness and endurance. The health metrics are deeper, especially for cardio training, sleep, recovery, and long‑term trends. The battery life is in a completely different league—days or even weeks depending on the model. It’s less slick as a smartwatch and more focused on training and performance.\n\nFitbit is simpler and affordable. It covers steps, sleep, heart rate, and basic stress tracking well, and it’s easy to use. But the ecosystem has shrunk a bit in depth compared to Apple and Garmin, and smart features are limited. It’s comfortable if you just want health basics and don’t care about lots of apps.\n\nSamsung Galaxy Watches sit between Apple and Fitbit in feel. On Android, they’re the best option for convenience, notifications, and everyday smart features. Health tracking is good, though not as deep as Garmin. Battery is better than Apple but still not multi‑day for heavy users.\n\nIf your priority is the most accurate, detailed health and fitness data, Garmin is usually the safest bet. If you want a smooth, convenient smartwatch that makes everyday tasks easy, go with Apple if you have an iPhone or Samsung if you have Android. If you want something simple and comfortable that won’t overwhelm you, Fitbit still works well.\n\nA good way to decide is to ask whether you want a tiny smart phone on your wrist or a dedicated health and fitness device. The ecosystems split pretty cleanly along that line.\n[user] I’m on an iPhone right now and mostly care about sleep quality, daily activity, and heart health trends, but I also don’t want to charge something every single night. How big is the real-world gap between Apple Watch and Garmin for battery and health insights, and are there any “gotchas” with Garmin day-to-day (notifications, payments, syncing, etc.)?\n[assistant] If you’re on an iPhone, the tradeoff between Apple Watch and Garmin is basically convenience versus endurance.\n\nApple Watch gives you the smoothest experience. Everything syncs perfectly, notifications come through reliably, and things like Apple Pay and music control feel effortless. Health trends—especially heart rate, cardio fitness, sleep stages, and long‑term patterns—are quite good now. The downside is still battery life. Most people need to charge it every day, or every day and a half if they’re light users. You can make it work with a quick charge before bed or after waking up, but it’s still a routine you have to think about.\n\nGarmin flips that around. Battery life is dramatically better. Depending on the model, you can go anywhere from four or five days to well over a week. If you want sleep tracking without worrying about power, it’s a big win. Garmin’s health insights are deeper when it comes to sleep quality, HRV, recovery, resting heart rate trends, and general “how is my body doing?” context. You feel less like you’re getting raw data and more like you’re getting interpretation.\n\nThe main “gotchas” with Garmin on an iPhone are small but noticeable. Notifications come through, but you can’t respond to messages from the watch. Apple Pay isn’t supported, so Garmin Pay becomes hit‑or‑miss depending on your bank. The watch feels more like a companion than an extension of your phone. Syncing is generally fine, but it’s not as instant or invisible as with Apple devices. And if you care about third‑party apps, Garmin’s selection is very limited.\n\nDay to day, Apple Watch is the more polished smartwatch, while Garmin is the better health tracker with far less charging stress. The question is whether you want tighter phone integration or a watch that quietly captures health data without demanding daily attention.\n[user] I’ve been wearing this smartwatch daily for the past year because it syncs well with my routine. If I switch to a Garmin, will I feel like I’m giving up that “it just works” syncing day-to-day on iPhone (especially for sleep/activity/heart trends), or is it close enough once it’s set up?\n[assistant] Once it’s set up, Garmin is generally “close enough” for health syncing on an iPhone, but it won’t feel quite as invisible as what you’re used to now.\n\nSleep, activity, and heart metrics all sync reliably into Garmin Connect, and the app is good at turning that data into meaningful trends. The syncing isn’t flaky; it’s just not as instantaneous or automatic as Apple’s background sync. With Apple Watch, data flows into your phone and Health app without you thinking about it. With Garmin, it syncs when the watch and phone reconnect, or when you open the Garmin app, which is usually fine but doesn’t feel as deeply integrated.\n\nYou also lose that sense of your watch being an extension of the phone. Notifications come through, but you can’t reply. Payments aren’t as universal. Some things require opening Garmin Connect instead of having everything live in the Apple Health app.\n\nIn practice, most people adjust after a few days. If your main goal is reliable health tracking and longer battery life, Garmin feels stable and consistent. It just doesn’t have that Apple “invisible glue” between devices. If you’re okay with the experience feeling slightly more manual but still dependable, you won’t feel like you’ve given up much.\n[user] If I want to keep most of my health data consolidated in Apple Health, how well does Garmin play with that on iPhone? Does it write sleep/HR/steps reliably into Apple Health, or do you basically end up living in Garmin Connect?\n[assistant] Garmin can write its data into Apple Health, and it generally does it reliably, but it’s not as clean or automatic as data coming from an Apple Watch.\n\nOnce you turn on permissions, Garmin will send over steps, heart rate, sleep, and other basics without much trouble. The catch is that the timing isn’t instant. Syncing usually happens when the watch connects to your phone or when you open the Garmin Connect app, and only after that does the data flow into Apple Health. It works, but it’s a step removed.\n\nSleep data also comes through, but Apple Health doesn’t display it as neatly as Garmin’s own app, so most people end up checking sleep details in Garmin Connect anyway. The deeper insights—Body Battery, training readiness, recovery scores—don’t transfer at all, so those stay inside Garmin’s ecosystem.\n\nIf your goal is to keep a unified history in Apple Health, Garmin can feed the essentials reliably enough. But for day‑to‑day understanding, you’ll still spend most of your time in Garmin Connect because that’s where the richer context lives.\n[user] Got it. If I go Garmin, which specific models are the best fit if I care more about sleep/HRV trends and everyday activity than hardcore training—and I still want decent iPhone notifications and at least 5–7 days of battery?\n[assistant] For what you care about, the sweet spots in Garmin’s lineup are the Venu 3, the Forerunner 265, and the vívoactive 5. All three focus more on health and daily use than on heavy endurance training, and all easily hit the 5–7‑day battery range.\n\nThe Venu 3 is the most balanced for everyday life. It has the best sleep and HRV tracking Garmin offers, strong recovery insights, and a bright AMOLED screen that feels more “smartwatch‑like.” Notifications on iPhone are reliable, and the battery usually lasts a full week.\n\nThe Forerunner 265 is lighter and a bit sportier, but still excellent for sleep, HRV, and general activity. The interface is a little more training‑oriented, but you don’t have to use the athletic features. Battery is usually around a week.\n\nThe vívoactive 5 is the affordable option. It has most of the same health tracking (including HRV and sleep scores) in a simpler package. Battery life is also in the one‑week zone. You lose some premium touches, but the core health features are strong.\n\nIf you want something that feels close to a smartwatch while still being a great health tracker, the Venu 3 is the safest choice. If you prefer lighter and sportier, go 265. If you want to save money without losing the essentials, the vívoactive 5 is the “quietly excellent” pick.\n</evidence_session_01>\n\n<evidence_session_02>\n[user] I’ve been thinking about how much I rely on my phone for basic day-to-day stuff like keeping track of time. Do you think dedicated gadgets still make sense in 2026, or is it basically always better to just use a smartphone?\n[assistant] I think dedicated gadgets can still make sense, but only when they genuinely do something better than a phone. For most everyday tasks—time, reminders, quick notes—a smartphone is just too convenient to beat.\n\nThat said, some devices still earn their place. Good watches, e‑readers, high‑end cameras, or fitness trackers can offer a nicer experience, better battery life, or fewer distractions than a phone ever will. It really comes down to whether the standalone device adds enough value that you’d actually enjoy using it instead of your phone.\n\nSo it’s not that phones replace everything; they just replace everything that isn’t meaningfully better on its own.\n[user] Ever since I switched to this simple analog wristwatch a few months ago, I’ve liked how it keeps me on time without any apps or charging. With that in mind, what other dedicated gadgets (if any) do you think are actually worth using mainly because they reduce distractions rather than add features?\n[assistant] If you’re enjoying the analog‑watch vibe—something that quietly does its job without dragging you into a notification spiral—there are a few other gadgets that tend to work well for that same reason.\n\nA Kindle‑style e‑reader is one of the big ones. It’s basically just for reading, the screen is calm, and there’s nothing buzzing at you. People who switch often say they read more simply because the temptations of a phone aren’t there.\n\nA dedicated music player can also be nice if you like listening without your phone pulling you into messages or apps. Even something as simple as a tiny clip‑on player feels surprisingly freeing if you use it for workouts or walks.\n\nSome folks also love having a basic alarm clock instead of using their phone at the bedside. It keeps the phone out of arm’s reach at night, which cuts down on late‑night scrolling and first‑thing‑in‑the‑morning doomchecking.\n\nAnd for note‑taking, a paper notebook or a simple digital notepad can be great. You write, you’re done, and there’s nothing else competing for your attention.\n\nNone of these are “necessary,” but they can protect your attention in a way a phone rarely does. If a device makes your day feel quieter or more focused, it’s usually worth keeping around.\n[user] If I wanted to try just one of those next—either an e‑reader or a basic alarm clock—which do you think would make the bigger difference day-to-day for cutting down on phone time?\n[assistant] Between the two, the alarm clock usually makes the bigger day‑to‑day impact. It forces your phone out of your bedroom routine, which is when most people accidentally rack up a lot of screen time. If your phone isn’t the thing you reach for right before sleep or right when you wake up, your overall usage naturally drops.\n\nAn e‑reader is great too, but it mostly changes your reading habits. A standalone alarm clock changes two big daily touchpoints with your phone, so the effect tends to be stronger and more immediate.\n[user] That makes sense. Any tips for setting up a basic alarm clock routine so I don’t just grab my phone anyway—like where to put the phone at night, what to use for a morning routine, and whether a “dumb” clock is better than a smart speaker?\n[assistant] A simple approach works best. Put your phone somewhere you’d have to physically get out of bed to reach—on a dresser across the room or even in the hallway. The goal isn’t to lock it away forever, just to break the reflex of grabbing it the moment you wake up or the moment you’re bored at night.\n\nA “dumb” alarm clock is usually better than a smart speaker because it can’t tempt you with voice commands, news briefings, or music. You set the time, it rings, and that’s it. That tiny bit of friction helps a lot.\n\nFor the morning routine, try giving yourself one small task before you touch your phone, like getting dressed, brushing your teeth, or making coffee. It creates a natural buffer so you start the day on your own terms instead of diving straight into notifications.\n\nOnce those pieces are in place, you barely have to think about it—the routine handles the discipline for you.\n</evidence_session_02>\n\nUser question:\nBased on the conversation history, does the user still wear a smartwatch every day?\n\nReturn only one JSON object with exactly these three fields:\n{\n  \"old_state\": \"the earlier relevant state supported by the sessions\",\n  \"new_state\": \"the later relevant state supported by the sessions\",\n  \"change_type\": \"CO_REFERENTIAL | PROPAGATED | UNKNOWN\"\n}\n\nRequirements:\n- old_state and new_state must be non-empty strings.\n- Use CO_REFERENTIAL when the same attribute changes directly.\n- Use PROPAGATED when one observed change indirectly invalidates a related state.\n- Use UNKNOWN when the relationship cannot be established from the sessions.\n- Do not add fields, code fences, or prose outside the JSON object."}], "temperature": 0, "top_p": 1, "max_tokens": 256, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}, "chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": false}, "stream": false}